1
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:14,450
Welcome to a training module provided by Front Range Nuclear Services.

2
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:18,450
From mobile solutions to full in-house services to our award-winning

3
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:22,450
Radiation Safety Officer and professional executive staff,

4
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:26,450
Front Range Nuclear Services has provided high-quality diagnostic imaging

5
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:29,450
to its customers since 1993.

6
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:33,450
Whether nuclear medicine, MRI, or any other imaging modality,

7
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:37,450
please let us know what we can do to better serve your community.

8
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:52,200
Nuclear medicine is a highly regulated modality that requires

9
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,200
extensive oversight, compliance with federal and state regulations,

10
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,200
and routine safety training of personnel.

11
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:03,200
That's why we're here today. That's why radiation safety training is necessary.

12
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:07,200
It is a state requirement that we inform as many people as practical

13
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,200
that might have some exposure to nuclear medicine.

14
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:17,440
So how do these radioactive isotopes or radioactive patients affect you?

15
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:21,440
The person has to work around these people or these isotopes

16
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:24,440
from time to time or even on a daily basis.

17
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:26,440
Well, let's take a look at some numbers.

18
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:31,440
First, it's important to understand that through natural resources alone,

19
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:35,440
such as the air we breathe, the sun that we receive every day,

20
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,440
composite materials, even elevation or flying in an aircraft,

21
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:43,440
all of these things produce a certain amount of radiation.

22
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:47,440
The average American receives about 360 millirem per year

23
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,440
from these sources alone.

24
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:52,440
Now, millirem may be a number that we're not familiar with,

25
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:54,440
but let's try to put that number in perspective.

26
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:58,440
As we understand that we receive about 360 millirem per year

27
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,440
from these sources alone, let's take it a little bit further.

28
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,440
For those people maybe that work with the treadmill

29
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:09,440
in nuclear medicine patients when nuclear medicine is being administered.

30
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:13,440
Large hospitals that have very high patient volume

31
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:18,440
putting through several patients per day have recorded up to 75 millirem

32
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:22,440
per year exposure for their treadmill staff.

33
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:26,440
Those numbers are considered very low levels of exposure.

34
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:28,440
Let's take it a little bit further.

35
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:31,440
How about those people that work with these radioactive isotopes

36
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:34,440
on a daily basis, such as a nuclear medicine technologist?

37
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:37,440
What are the maximum limits?

38
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:41,440
For whole body, there's 5,000 millirem per year

39
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,440
is the maximum allowable annual dose

40
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:48,440
for those that work with nuclear medicine on a daily basis.

41
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:51,440
There's another number for their extremities, the hands,

42
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,440
and that's 50,000 millirem per year max.

43
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:57,440
As we look at the whole big picture,

44
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:02,440
we're starting to get a perspective of maximum allowable dose limits

45
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:04,440
for those that work with nuclear medicine

46
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:07,440
and the amounts that we receive just from living

47
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:10,440
and breathing and walking on the earth.

48
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,440
Let's take it a little bit further.

49
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:15,440
Pregnant technologists are actually allowed

50
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,440
to continue performing nuclear medicine

51
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:20,440
and performing their job as a technologist,

52
00:03:20,440 --> 00:03:23,440
and they are allowed 500 millirem exposure

53
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,440
during the gestational period.

54
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:28,440
They're allowed to continue doing their job

55
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:31,440
provided they stay with those limits.

56
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,440
All these levels that we're discussing here

57
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,440
are considered safe annual doses for radiation workers.

58
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:43,060
But what about the technologists?

59
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:46,060
How much do they really receive per year?

60
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:48,060
Most nuclear medicine techs receive

61
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:51,060
about 200 millirem per year exposure

62
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:54,060
as monitored by their badge devices.

63
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:57,060
These badge devices are required to be worn

64
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:00,060
by nuclear medicine technologists both on their hand

65
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:04,060
and on their body so that we can record

66
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:07,060
their whole body exposure and their extremity exposure

67
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:12,060
and count what their total millirem per year exposure is

68
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:17,060
over the span of the year to ensure that they are being safe.

69
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:19,060
Those who have casual contact

70
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:25,060
receive a fraction of the safe annual doses listed above.

71
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:27,060
Let's take a quick look at the different types

72
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:30,060
of ionizing radiation.

73
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:34,060
The first is alpha particles.

74
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,060
Alpha particles can simply be protected

75
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:40,060
by something as thin as a sheet of paper.

76
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:42,060
A sheet of paper actually stops those particles

77
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:45,060
from passing through.

78
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:47,060
Then we have beta particles.

79
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:50,060
Beta particles are stopped by your layer of clothing

80
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:54,060
or less than an inch of any other substance.

81
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,060
And gamma rays.

82
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:58,060
Gamma rays, the majority of which,

83
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:02,060
are stopped by inches up to feet of concrete

84
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:05,060
or less than an inch of lead.

85
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:09,060
That's why lead is often used with radioactive isotopes

86
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:11,060
and for the protection of the people

87
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:16,590
that work around those materials.

88
00:05:16,590 --> 00:05:18,590
To help protect us against these types

89
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:20,590
of ionizing radiation,

90
00:05:20,590 --> 00:05:25,590
we use the time, distance, and shielding concept.

91
00:05:25,590 --> 00:05:30,590
Time, you'll want to decrease your time near the source.

92
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:36,290
Distance is to increase your distance

93
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:40,290
from the source of radiation.

94
00:05:40,290 --> 00:05:44,290
And shielding, to protect yourself by way of a shield,

95
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:47,290
whether it's the inches to feet of concrete

96
00:05:47,290 --> 00:05:51,290
or walls or lead shielding.

97
00:05:51,290 --> 00:05:55,290
This is all part of the Alera concept,

98
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:58,290
which is what we're going to discuss next.

99
00:05:58,290 --> 00:06:01,290
Alera is a concept that you might have heard before

100
00:06:01,290 --> 00:06:04,290
and you might be familiar with, but let's review.

101
00:06:04,290 --> 00:06:10,290
Alera simply means as low, as reasonably achievable.

102
00:06:10,290 --> 00:06:12,290
It's kind of a common sense approach

103
00:06:12,290 --> 00:06:17,790
to help protect a person from ionizing radiation.

104
00:06:17,790 --> 00:06:19,790
The three things that we just discussed,

105
00:06:19,790 --> 00:06:21,790
time, distance, and shielding,

106
00:06:21,790 --> 00:06:25,790
are the three key elements as part of the Alera concept.

107
00:06:25,790 --> 00:06:29,810
Another part of the Alera concept

108
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:32,810
is the methods of wipes and surveys.

109
00:06:32,810 --> 00:06:34,810
Our technologists are trained

110
00:06:34,810 --> 00:06:37,810
in the area of wipes and surveys.

111
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:40,810
They use specialized equipment and procedures

112
00:06:40,810 --> 00:06:43,810
when they test different rooms

113
00:06:43,810 --> 00:06:46,810
to see if there is any unnecessary exposures,

114
00:06:46,810 --> 00:06:49,810
drips, or spills within any of the rooms

115
00:06:49,810 --> 00:06:52,810
where we perform nuclear medicine studies.

116
00:06:52,810 --> 00:06:55,810
We also, as part of the Alera concept,

117
00:06:55,810 --> 00:06:59,810
have spill protocols and containment protocols,

118
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:01,810
but a spill can be something as small

119
00:07:01,810 --> 00:07:04,810
as just a couple of drops of radioactivity,

120
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:07,810
and containment is exactly what it means.

121
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:11,810
If there is a spill, our employees are highly trained

122
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:14,810
in containing that spill to help ensure

123
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:16,810
that the radioactivity is not spread

124
00:07:16,810 --> 00:07:19,810
and contaminating other areas.

125
00:07:19,810 --> 00:07:23,810
Also, the use of PPE is another important part

126
00:07:24,810 --> 00:07:29,910
to the Alera concept.

127
00:07:29,910 --> 00:07:31,910
Occasionally, you have a patient that's concerned

128
00:07:31,910 --> 00:07:34,910
about the amount of radiation that they're receiving,

129
00:07:34,910 --> 00:07:37,910
so what do you tell these patients about nuclear medicine?

130
00:07:37,910 --> 00:07:40,910
Well, it's important to note that nuclear medicine

131
00:07:40,910 --> 00:07:43,910
is proven to be very safe for use in children,

132
00:07:43,910 --> 00:07:46,910
adults, and even in pregnant women,

133
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:49,910
and that the exposure to radioactivity

134
00:07:49,910 --> 00:07:51,910
is considered extremely low.

135
00:07:51,910 --> 00:07:53,910
It's also important to note

136
00:07:53,910 --> 00:07:56,910
that the radiation used is in liquid form,

137
00:07:56,910 --> 00:07:58,910
and it is injected into the bloodstream.

138
00:07:58,910 --> 00:08:02,910
They are not being irradiated by any other means.

139
00:08:02,910 --> 00:08:04,910
While they are being imaged by the camera,

140
00:08:04,910 --> 00:08:08,910
the camera itself is not producing the radiation.

141
00:08:08,910 --> 00:08:11,910
It's also important to note that common exposure

142
00:08:11,910 --> 00:08:15,910
is equivalent to receiving about two chest x-rays,

143
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:20,910
or it's also equivalent to about spending 20 days in the sun.

144
00:08:21,910 --> 00:08:24,910
Also, in most cases,

145
00:08:24,910 --> 00:08:27,910
the radiation that is received by the liquid injection

146
00:08:27,910 --> 00:08:29,910
is completely gone from the body

147
00:08:29,910 --> 00:08:32,910
within 48 hours of injection.

148
00:08:32,910 --> 00:08:35,910
This information is helpful for those patients

149
00:08:35,910 --> 00:08:36,910
that might be a little nervous

150
00:08:36,910 --> 00:08:39,910
about their nuclear medicine study.

151
00:08:39,910 --> 00:08:45,540
As with almost any medical procedure,

152
00:08:45,540 --> 00:08:47,540
there are those patients that will wind up

153
00:08:47,540 --> 00:08:49,540
needing emergency care.

154
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:51,540
Let's talk about nuclear medicine patients

155
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:54,540
and how to handle an emergency.

156
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,540
First, it is important to note

157
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:59,540
that all excretions are radioactive.

158
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:02,540
However, the amount of radioactivity

159
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:05,540
is considered to be extremely small.

160
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:09,540
There is, in fact, more concern about blood-borne pathogens

161
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:12,540
than there is a concern about the amount of radioactivity

162
00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:14,540
coming from that person.

163
00:09:14,540 --> 00:09:17,940
It's important to note that even a person

164
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:20,940
that has been injected with radioactive isotope

165
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:23,940
can never delay life-saving techniques.

166
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:26,940
The risks involved with the radioactivity

167
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:28,940
are extremely small.

168
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:33,940
Also, after touching a patient where excretions are present,

169
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:36,940
whether it be urine or blood,

170
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:38,940
or that excretions were suspected,

171
00:09:38,940 --> 00:09:40,940
ensure that you avoid touching other objects,

172
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:43,940
such as doorknobs or computers or anything else,

173
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:46,940
and avoid going into other areas

174
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:52,940
without being cleared by a nuclear medicine technologist first.

175
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:55,940
It's important to note that once a person

176
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:57,940
that has received a nuclear medicine injection,

177
00:09:57,940 --> 00:09:59,940
once they have already completed their study

178
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:01,940
and have left the hospital,

179
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:04,940
it's unlikely that the hospital during an emergency situation

180
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:06,940
would know that that person had received

181
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:09,940
a radioactive injection.

182
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:12,940
PPE is an extremely important part

183
00:10:12,940 --> 00:10:16,940
and would normally be worn during an emergency situation

184
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:18,940
and should provide adequate protection

185
00:10:18,940 --> 00:10:21,940
against radioactivity.

186
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:24,940
It's also important to note that if there is an emergency situation

187
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:26,940
and there were excretions present,

188
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:29,940
that the technologist will collect all contaminated items

189
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:31,940
in a designated container,

190
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:37,940
and that is to prevent further contamination of radioactivity.

191
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:39,940
Following an emergency situation

192
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:43,940
involving a nuclear medicine patient, if possible,

193
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:45,940
all members present during the emergency situation

194
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:49,940
that may have been in contact with radioactive excretions,

195
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:52,940
please remain in the room until surveyed

196
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:56,940
and cleared by the nuclear medicine technologist.

197
00:10:56,940 --> 00:11:00,940
The technologist will be responsible for all cleanup

198
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:03,940
regarding radioactive materials.

199
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:07,940
It's also important to note that rooms may be temporarily closed

200
00:11:07,940 --> 00:11:10,940
depending on the level of contamination surveyed

201
00:11:10,940 --> 00:11:13,940
or found by the nuclear medicine technologist.

202
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:16,940
The room will be reopened once the room has been deemed

203
00:11:16,940 --> 00:11:19,940
completely clear of any radioactivity.

204
00:11:19,940 --> 00:11:25,290
In this next section, let's talk about how to handle a spill.

205
00:11:25,290 --> 00:11:41,070
But first, what is a spill?

206
00:11:41,070 --> 00:11:47,020
The video depicted the nuclear medicine technologist

207
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:51,020
accidentally dropping the radioactive isotope on the floor.

208
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:53,020
That's one possible scenario,

209
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:56,020
but let's talk about some other things first.

210
00:11:56,020 --> 00:11:59,020
First of all, the most likely location of a spill

211
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:01,020
would be in the treadmill room.

212
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:04,020
It's important to note that it may only be one drop

213
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:08,020
of radioactive isotope to be considered a spill.

214
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:11,020
If you're in the room, please remain calm.

215
00:12:11,020 --> 00:12:15,020
No one is in any immediate danger.

216
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:18,020
The nuclear medicine technologist will be providing

217
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:20,020
all the guidance for those that are in the room,

218
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:23,650
including for the physician.

219
00:12:23,650 --> 00:12:25,650
No matter how helpful you want to be,

220
00:12:25,650 --> 00:12:29,650
be sure not to reach in to help the nuclear medicine technologist.

221
00:12:29,650 --> 00:12:32,650
That's to prevent any further contamination.

222
00:12:32,650 --> 00:12:34,650
The nuclear medicine technologist will handle

223
00:12:34,650 --> 00:12:38,020
all aspects of the cleanup.

224
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:41,020
Also, remain in the room until you've been cleared

225
00:12:41,020 --> 00:12:44,020
by the technologist.

226
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:46,020
Be advised that the room may be closed

227
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:49,020
until the activity is clear.

228
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:52,020
It's important to note that remaining patients

229
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:56,020
on the schedule may be able to continue with their study.

230
00:12:56,020 --> 00:13:00,430
The nuclear medicine technologist will complete

231
00:13:00,430 --> 00:13:03,430
all surveys and do so under the supervision

232
00:13:03,430 --> 00:13:05,430
of the radiation safety officer

233
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:08,430
in accordance with state regulation.

234
00:13:08,430 --> 00:13:11,430
For some, the thought of nuclear medicine

235
00:13:11,430 --> 00:13:14,430
and radioactivity is a scary thought.

236
00:13:14,430 --> 00:13:16,430
That's why we are staffed with highly trained

237
00:13:16,430 --> 00:13:18,430
nuclear medicine technologist

238
00:13:18,430 --> 00:13:21,430
and an award-winning radiation safety officer

239
00:13:21,430 --> 00:13:23,430
to ensure that we are always handling

240
00:13:23,430 --> 00:13:25,430
all regulatory issues

241
00:13:25,430 --> 00:13:28,430
so the hospital can concentrate on other matters.

242
00:13:28,430 --> 00:13:31,430
Some other points of interest are

243
00:13:31,430 --> 00:13:34,430
regarding those patients that need lab work,

244
00:13:34,430 --> 00:13:37,430
it would be best to have lab work completed

245
00:13:37,430 --> 00:13:39,430
prior to a nuclear medicine study,

246
00:13:39,430 --> 00:13:42,430
or if the lab work cannot be completed

247
00:13:42,430 --> 00:13:44,430
prior to the nuclear medicine study,

248
00:13:44,430 --> 00:13:46,430
to wait at least 24 hours

249
00:13:46,430 --> 00:13:48,430
following a nuclear medicine study

250
00:13:48,430 --> 00:13:50,430
to get lab work completed.

251
00:13:50,430 --> 00:13:52,430
Another point of interest is that

252
00:13:52,430 --> 00:13:54,430
nuclear medicine technologists should be the ones

253
00:13:54,430 --> 00:13:57,430
to remove and dispose of IVs.

254
00:13:57,430 --> 00:14:00,430
The exception to that is for in-house patients.

255
00:14:00,430 --> 00:14:04,430
In-house patients may keep their IVs as necessary.

256
00:14:04,430 --> 00:14:06,430
The IV will be flushed

257
00:14:06,430 --> 00:14:08,430
and the majority of the radioactivity

258
00:14:08,430 --> 00:14:10,430
will be in the bloodstream

259
00:14:10,430 --> 00:14:14,160
and not in the IV itself.

260
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:16,160
This concludes our short training module

261
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:19,160
on radiation safety.

262
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:21,160
Our nuclear medicine technologist

263
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:23,160
will keep themselves available

264
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:25,160
for any questions that you may have

265
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:29,530
about the training that you receive today.

266
00:14:29,530 --> 00:14:31,530
Keep front-range nuclear services in mind

267
00:14:31,530 --> 00:14:33,530
